  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 309 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nWarm and humid air will spread back into southern WI on\nsoutherly winds Friday as a low pressure trough approaches from\nthe Northern Plains. Showers and thunderstorms are possible\nalong this front as it tracks through southern WI Friday night.\nThe timing of this front and also an inconsistency with \nlow/upper level forcing and some mid level dry air keep our\nchances for storms low, especially our severe chances. \n\nA northerly breeze is expected Saturday in the wake of this\nfront, with a weak lake breeze likely. Quiet weather with highs\nin the lower 80s and dewpoints in the lower 60s will make for a\nnice day.\n\nSaturday night will begin a period of unsettled weather as we\nget into mid level zonal flow due to broad high pressure over\nthe southwest U.S. and low pressure near Hudson Bay. Weak\nripples in the flow (mid level shortwave troughs) will cause\nperiods of thunderstorms, or potentially thunderstorm \ncomplexes, to develop and track through southern WI. The first\nmain chance of storms that models are signaling should develop\non the nose of the low level jet over Iowa that could track\nacross southern WI and northern IL late Saturday night into \nSunday morning. Depending on the strength of that complex,\nstrong winds could be possible, but it is not quite in the\nforecast range for predictability due to the weaker nature of\nthe upper wave.\n\nThe next shortwave that has consistency between the models is\nexpected to develop in the Dakotas Sunday night and track \nacross WI by midday Monday. We will need to watch our severe\nchances with this one given the time of day. \n\nStorm chances for southern WI beyond Monday will depend on how\nMonday plays out, but models are favoring the effective front to\nbe south of WI by Tuesday. If the front can nudge back\nnorthward, then our storm chances will return at times for the\nlatter half of next week. \n\nCronce",
